## What Happened

Apple removed DSA from its native cryptography frameworks (Security.framework / CryptoKit). .NET on macOS delegates to the platform's crypto libraries, so `DSA.Create()` now throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS. Linux (OpenSSL) and Windows (CNG) still support DSA, which is why only macOS breaks.

## How to Fix

**Option 1 – Use BouncyCastle (managed, cross-platform):**

```csharp
// Install: dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

bool VerifyDsa(byte[] message, byte[] signature, DsaPublicKeyParameters pubKey)
{
    var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA1withDSA");
    verifier.Init(false, pubKey);
    verifier.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
    return verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

**Option 2 – Conditionally use OpenSSL on macOS:**

Bundle or reference OpenSSL on macOS (e.g., via Homebrew's `libssl`) and P/Invoke into it, though this adds deployment complexity.

**Recommendation:** BouncyCastle's managed implementation is the simplest path — no platform dependencies, works identically on all three OSes, and handles legacy DSA verification without issues.
